\begin{abstract}
Quantum spin ice has a well-established long-wavelength description in
terms of an emergent photon, while previous exact-diagonalization and
semiclassical studies already show that the underlying compact gauge
theory is strongly interacting. What is not fixed by those results is
how the microscopic ring model distributes neutron spectral weight at
finite momentum. We compute the zero-temperature
$\Szz(\bq,\omega)$ of the pure ring-exchange model in the ground-state
winding sector of 48- and 64-site pyrochlore clusters. The response is
photon-like at the smallest accessible momentum but reconstructs at the
zone boundary. At $L$ a neutron-active collective level appears at
$1.02K$ on 48 sites ($0.95K$ on 64 sites), carries approximately half
of the total intensity, and lies at about half the Gaussian-photon
energy. We call this mode roton-like in the Feynman sense: it is
continuously connected to a transverse photon branch and is softened
where the ratio of exact oscillator strength to equal-time structure
factor, $f_1(\bq)/S(\bq)$, is minimal. A standard Rokhsar--Kivelson
flippability potential, used only as an infinitesimal probe at $V=0$,
shows that this level gains flippable hexagons while losing
ring-exchange coherence, opposite to the ordinary photon branch. The
same spectra show an order-one interaction-induced polarization
splitting at the zone boundary and transfer $13$--$38\%$ of neutron
weight outside the photon branches. These results resolve the
finite-momentum strong-coupling structure hidden by a Gaussian photon
description.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\emph{Introduction.}---Quantum spin ice realizes a compact $U(1)$
gauge theory inside a frustrated magnet. In the two-in--two-out
manifold of the pyrochlore lattice, virtual exchange flips the six spins
around an elementary hexagon~\cite{Castelnovo2008,Hermele2004}. The
resulting deconfined phase supports a gapless emergent photon together
with gapped electric and magnetic charges~\cite{Gingras2014,
SavaryBalents2017,RauGingras2019,Broholm2020}. Because the local moment
is the emergent electric field, $S^z=E$, neutron scattering measures
electric-field fluctuations through $\Szz(\bq,\omega)$. The
long-wavelength photon and its characteristic correlations have been
established by gauge theory, quantum Monte Carlo, and constrained-space
numerics~\cite{Hermele2004,Benton2012,Shannon2012,KatoOnoda2015,
Pace2021} and motivate searches in a growing set of candidate
materials~\cite{Ross2011,Sibille2018,Gaudet2019,Gao2019,Sibille2020,
Smith2022,Poree2025,Gao2025,Gao2026}.

The microscopic problem is nevertheless not weakly interacting.
Gaussian treatments expand the compact magnetic energy about small
flux and give a non-compact Maxwell theory whose neutron response is
concentrated in one-photon poles~\cite{Hermele2004,Benton2012,
Desrochers2023,DesrochersKim2023PRL}. This description is controlled
in the infrared, but several results already show that it becomes
incomplete at shorter wavelength. The emergent fine-structure
constant is large~\cite{Pace2021}; semiclassical expansions find strong
anharmonic renormalization and, in particular, an interaction-induced
splitting of the two transverse photon polarizations at intermediate
wave vector~\cite{Kwasigroch2017,Kwasigroch2020,Szabo2019}; and quantum
Monte Carlo with analytic continuation finds substantial
zone-boundary broadening not captured by Gaussian electrodynamics
~\cite{Huang2018}. Constrained-space exact diagonalization has also
been used to determine low-energy spectra and extract emergent QED
parameters on clusters considerably larger than those used here
~\cite{Pace2021}.

The unresolved question we address is therefore narrower: \emph{how is
the neutron spectral weight of the microscopic ring model distributed
among its finite-energy eigenstates at finite momentum?} Analytic
continuation can reveal broad dynamical features but not the individual
finite-size poles, matrix elements, and state character needed to
answer this question sharply, while previous exact diagonalization was
organized around low-energy QED parameters rather than the complete
operator-resolved response. We compute the matrix elements of
$S^z(\bq)$ directly in the constrained Hilbert space. The result is a
long-wavelength photon that survives as expected and a zone-boundary
spectrum that is strongly reconstructed. The most prominent feature
is an anomalously soft $L$-point level carrying about half of the
neutron intensity. We will call it \emph{roton-like}: this terminology
does not claim that our sparse finite-size momenta establish a local
minimum of a thermodynamic dispersion. It denotes a finite-momentum
collective continuation of a photon branch whose softening follows the
Feynman ratio $f_1/S$ and whose microscopic response differs
qualitatively from that of the long-wavelength photon.

\emph{Model and scope.}---We study the standard pyrochlore
ring-exchange Hamiltonian within the spin-ice manifold
~\cite{Hermele2004,Shannon2012},
\begin{equation}
 H=-K\sum_{p}\left(W_{p}+W_{p}^{\dagger}\right),\qquad
 W_{p}=S_1^+S_2^-S_3^+S_4^-S_5^+S_6^-,
 \label{eq:ring}
\end{equation}
summed over contractible elementary hexagons. $W_p$ reverses an
alternating six-spin configuration and annihilates a nonflippable one.
Thus the kinetic energy rewards coherent resonance among flippable
hexagons. In gauge language $W_p=e^{iB_p}$ and
Eq.~(\ref{eq:ring}) is the compact magnetic energy
$-2K\sum_p\cos B_p$.

For spin one-half there is no independent bare electric stiffness
within the ice manifold. In the conventional compact-gauge
Hamiltonian $\frac{U}{2}\sum_\ell E_\ell^2-K\sum_p\cos B_p$, the
identification $E_\ell=S^z_\ell=\pm\frac12$ makes
$E_\ell^2=\frac14$ a constant. The photon stiffness is therefore
generated dynamically by the ring term itself
~\cite{Hermele2004,Benton2012,Kwasigroch2017}. This is the strongly
interacting limit in which we ask how the microscopic spectral weight
departs from Gaussian electrodynamics.

Our calculation is exact within the specified finite Hilbert spaces,
not within the full microscopic spin model. Equation~(\ref{eq:ring})
conserves winding, and we work in the ground-state winding sector of
the spin-ice manifold. Electric charges (spinons) are absent by
construction. The magnetic sector is retained, so spectral weight
outside the photon branches could in principle have magnetic rather
than multiphoton character; we distinguish these possibilities below.
We also omit noncontractible six-cycles that generate torus artifacts
on small clusters~\cite{ZhouKimMask}.

\emph{Operator-resolved spectrum.}---The neutron operator has an
especially direct gauge interpretation. Since $S^z=E$, the state
$S^z(\bq)|0\rangle$ is the electric-field wave launched by the probe.
In Gaussian electrodynamics this state lies entirely in the one-photon
sector. In the compact microscopic model it can overlap with every
odd-parity eigenstate allowed by the same quantum numbers. Resolving
those overlaps, rather than only the lowest energy in each momentum
sector, is the central numerical task here.

The two gauge fields also have transparent spin meanings. $E_\ell$ is
the Ising moment on bond $\ell$, whereas $B_p$ is the flux through a
hexagon. The expectation value
$\langle W_p+W_p^\dagger\rangle=2\langle\cos B_p\rangle$ measures the
coherence of the six-spin ring resonance. Since
Eq.~(\ref{eq:ring}) contains the entire nonconstant energy in the ice
manifold, changes in this coherence provide a direct energetic
diagnostic of the excited states.

