\emph{Why the softening occurs at $L$.}---Two ingredients coincide at
this momentum. The first is geometric. A hexagon contained in a
wavefront, i.e. in a plane of constant phase perpendicular to $\bq$,
encloses zero flux from the electric-field wave: the alternating loop
sum defining $g_p$ vanishes exactly. Such a plaquette contributes
nothing to Eq.~(\ref{eq:f1}). Pyrochlore hexagons are perpendicular
to the four $\langle111\rangle$ directions, and at the sampled
zone-boundary point $L\parallel[111]$ one of the four hexagon families
lies in the wavefronts. That family therefore drops out of the
oscillator-strength cost exactly. On the cluster this reduces
$f_1(L)$ to three quarters of the corresponding value for neighboring
zone-boundary orientations.

The second ingredient is dynamical. In the interacting $V=0$ ground
state the equal-time structure factor is largest at $L$ among the
sampled momenta: $S(L)=0.370$, compared with $0.19$--$0.29$ elsewhere.
The combination of reduced $f_1$ and enhanced $S$ makes
$f_1/S$ smallest at $L$ among the twelve cluster momenta and makes it
the only sampled point where the single-mode energy falls below the
Gaussian-photon line.

Crucially, the enhancement of $S(L)$ is not inherited from the ice
constraint alone. Direct sampling of a 2048-site classical ice
manifold gives a structure factor nearly flat across the zone, and the
equal-amplitude ice state on the finite cluster enhances $L$ by only
about ten percent. The much stronger $L$ enhancement is generated by
the quantum ring dynamics at $V=0$. This is the main distinction from
an RK-point resonon, whose softening can be read directly from the
classical correlations of the equal-amplitude state.

\emph{Microscopic character of the low $L$ mode.}---The state-resolved
expectation values show that flippability and resonance coherence are
different observables. Relative to the ground state, the low $L$
level contains $0.34$ more flippable hexagons, yet its total magnetic
coherence $\sum_p\langle\cos B_p\rangle$ is smaller. Because the ring
term is the full nonconstant Hamiltonian in the constrained manifold,
the loss of coherence accounts directly for the excitation energy.
In spin language, more plaquettes are instantaneously arranged so that
they \emph{could} flip, while the phases of those ring processes are
less coherent. The mode therefore pays energy by disrupting
resonance, not by violating the ice rule.

The single-mode electric-field wave has expectation energy $1.45K$,
whereas the exact low $L$ eigenstate lies at $1.02K$. Thus variational
relaxation recovers $29\%$ of the bare single-mode cost. A
plaquette-resolved decomposition attributes $71\%$ of that recovery to
the hexagon family lying in the wavefronts. Those plaquettes carry no
flux from the imposed wave and can reorganize without directly
reducing its overlap with the neutron-created state. This is closely
analogous to backflow in Feynman--Cohen theory~\cite{FeynmanCohen1956}:
the collective excitation lowers its energy by rearranging degrees of
freedom not fixed by the bare density, here electric-field, wave.

\emph{Why ``roton-like.''}---The terminology is meant to identify a
mechanism and a change of character, not to overstate the momentum
resolution of the clusters. Projection onto the Gaussian transverse
polarizations connects the low $L$ level continuously to one photon
branch. It is therefore not a new elementary species. At the same
time, describing it merely as a photon with a renormalized velocity
misses the state-resolved diagnostics: the ordinary photon descendant
loses flippable plaquettes under the RK bias, whereas the low $L$ mode
gains them; the latter combines increased flippability with reduced
ring coherence; and its softening tracks the Feynman ratio rather than
a uniform rescaling of the photon band.

This is the sense in which the analogy to the helium roton is useful.
In helium the high-momentum continuation of the sound branch is
softened by correlations and backflow. Here the electric field is
microscopically saturated, $E=S^z=\pm\frac12$, so a finite-amplitude
wave cannot be formed by increasing the magnitude of $E$ on a bond.
It must instead rearrange which bonds carry the two allowed values.
That rearrangement changes the magnetic flux and the coherence of ring
exchange. The data show that at $L$ this correlated rearrangement is
substantially cheaper than the Gaussian continuation of the
long-wavelength photon. Establishing a true local minimum of the
thermodynamic dispersion would require denser momentum resolution than
our clusters provide; our claim is the finite-size, mechanism-based
one stated above.

\emph{Relation to RK resonons.}---Single-mode soft modes are familiar
in quantum dimer models at Rokhsar--Kivelson points. There the ground
state is an equal-amplitude superposition and its static correlations
are those of the corresponding classical dimer ensemble
~\cite{RokhsarKivelson1988}. On the square lattice this produces the
well-known resonon structure, confirmed in dynamical calculations
~\cite{Lauchli2008}. The three-dimensional cubic RK model is different:
its only soft mode is the remnant of the photon, consistent with the
comparatively featureless classical Coulomb-phase correlations
~\cite{Lauchli2008}. Our $L$ anomaly is therefore not an RK-point soft
mode transplanted to pyrochlore. It occurs at the physical $V=0$ ring
point, remains at finite energy on the available clusters, and relies
on an $S(L)$ enhancement generated by the interacting quantum ground
state rather than by the equal-amplitude classical ensemble.

\emph{Finite-size and deformation checks.}---Three internal checks
support a collective interpretation of the low $L$ level. First, a
systematic variational improvement of the electric-field wave converges
rapidly toward the exact 48-site eigenvalue,
$1.450\to1.074\to1.034\to1.026K$, compared with $1.024K$ from complete
diagonalization. Second, on the 64-site anisotropic cluster the two
inequivalent $L$-type momentum classes behave differently: the class
with larger $S(\bq)$ develops the low level carrying about half of the
neutron intensity, while the class with suppressed $S(\bq)$ remains
photon-dominated. Thus the effect follows the structure factor rather
than the label $L$ alone. Third, under a deformation toward more
flippable configurations, $f_1(L)$ changes by less than one percent
while $S(L)$ grows and the low mode softens from $1.02K$ to $0.73K$ over
the scanned interval. This correlated evolution is the behavior
expected from the $f_1/S$ mechanism.

\emph{Nonperturbative photon interactions.}---The remaining spectral
weight provides a second measure of strong coupling. The two
transverse polarizations are exactly degenerate in the Gaussian theory.
Their interaction-induced splitting was predicted previously in
semiclassical treatments~\cite{Kwasigroch2017,Kwasigroch2020}; our
calculation determines its nonperturbative magnitude in the microscopic
ring model. The splitting is below one percent at the smallest
accessible momentum and becomes order one at the zone boundary.
Beyond the two photon descendants, $13$--$38\%$ of the neutron weight
resides in higher levels absent from the Gaussian one-photon theory.
The off-pole fraction decreases to $13\%$ at the smallest sampled
momentum, consistent with recovery of the infrared photon.

Several diagnostics favor a multiparticle rather than a localized
magnetic interpretation of this higher weight. Plaquette-flux
correlations are weak and spatially extended instead of showing the
localized disturbance expected for a bound magnetic pair, and the
response to the RK deformation is consistent with sums of single-mode
responses. By contrast, leading-order perturbation theory in the
quartic term obtained by expanding the cosine predicts polarization
splittings and transferred spectral weight below $0.1\%$ at the
coupling generated by this model, and only a few percent even when that
coupling is artificially tripled. The finite-momentum spectrum is
therefore not quantitatively described as a weak anharmonic correction
to the Gaussian photon.

\emph{Parity and complementary probes.}---Global spin reversal leaves
Eq.~(\ref{eq:ring}) invariant. Eigenstates can therefore be chosen
even or odd under this symmetry, while $S^z$ is odd. Consequently a
spin-reversal-even ground state couples through neutron scattering only
to odd states. This elementary selection rule is exact in the model;
numerically every even level carries neutron weight below $10^{-28}$.
In gauge language it is charge conjugation, under which an $n$-photon
state has parity $(-1)^n$.

The practical implication is a clean division between linear and
bilinear probes. Even-sector weight, including the two-photon channel,
is invisible to $S^z$ but can appear in probes coupling to spin pairs,
such as Raman scattering~\cite{Fu2017} and ultrasound~\cite{Simon2022}.
Stray-field noise magnetometry~\cite{Naik2026}, which couples to
magnetization, belongs to the neutron-like odd channel. The selection
rule itself is not a new symmetry statement; its value here is that the
complete finite-size spectrum makes the complementary experimental
channels explicit.

