\emph{Experimental implications.}---The calculation does not imply
that every zone-boundary feature in a microscopic quantum-spin-ice
material is the same as the low $L$ level of the pure ring model.
Longer ring exchanges, virtual spinons, and material-specific
interactions can modify the spectrum. It does show, however, that
even the minimal compact gauge Hamiltonian can redistribute a large
fraction of neutron weight away from the Gaussian photon without
leaving the deconfined phase. At the sampled $L$ point the dominant
neutron-active level lies at roughly half the Gaussian-photon energy
and carries about half of the total intensity; the photon descendant
retains only a minority of the weight. More generally, the
zone-boundary response contains split photon branches and substantial
off-pole intensity.

This provides a microscopic candidate for the broad finite-momentum
response seen in quantum Monte Carlo~\cite{Huang2018}, but we do not
identify the QMC linewidth uniquely with the finite-size levels found
here. In the thermodynamic limit the gapless theory also permits
multi-quantum continua to extend down toward the one-photon branch, so
experimental comparison should emphasize the redistribution and
lineshape of spectral weight rather than a one-to-one count of finite
cluster poles.

\emph{Limitations and clusters.}---Our conclusions are subject to four
explicit restrictions. First, the calculation is performed in the
ground-state winding sector of the spin-ice ring model, not over all
topological sectors and not in the full microscopic spin Hilbert space.
Second, only two cluster sizes are available. The $L$-point energy,
its approximately one-half share of neutron weight, and its
flippability/structure-factor mechanism are stable between them, but
some other details, including the splitting at $X$, are not. Third,
the discrete cluster momenta do not establish a local minimum of a
continuous thermodynamic dispersion; this is why we use the qualified
term roton-like. A zone-wide prediction would require the interacting
$S(\bq)$ at momenta inaccessible to these clusters. Fourth, longer
ring exchanges and virtual-spinon processes in microscopic XXZ models
lie outside Eq.~(\ref{eq:ring}). A companion calculation with torus
artifacts removed finds comparable off-pole weight at $L$ in the XXZ
model but also virtual-spinon admixture in photon-band states
~\cite{ZhouKimMask}; a detailed comparison is deferred. The overall
ring scale $K$ can be constrained thermodynamically
~\cite{ZhouAnKim2026}.

The cluster choice itself is important. The standard 16- and 32-site
pyrochlore cells are pathological for this question. On the 32-site
cell every configuration in the connected zero-flux sector has exactly
eight flippable hexagons, making the equal-amplitude state an exact
eigenstate and producing an artificially integer-valued spectrum; on
the 16-site cell only six transport sectors have any dynamics. We
therefore use the $2{\times}2{\times}3$ cluster (48 sites,
$87\,546$ ice configurations; ground-state winding sector of dimension
$9104$), which we diagonalize completely so that the operator-resolved
weights exactly saturate the equal-time sum rule. For the size check
we use $2{\times}2{\times}4$ (64 sites, $2\,756\,394$ ice
configurations; ground-state winding sector of dimension $249\,180$)
and Lanczos after reproducing all 48-site benchmarks. Momentum labels
are certified by exact translation projectors.

\emph{Conclusion.}---The standard pyrochlore ring Hamiltonian is known
to realize a strongly interacting compact $U(1)$ gauge theory; our
result concerns how that strong coupling appears in its
operator-resolved finite-momentum spectrum. At long wavelength the
neutron response is dominated by the expected emergent photon. At the
sampled $L$ point, however, the spectral weight reconstructs around a
collective level at about half the Gaussian-photon energy carrying
approximately half of the intensity. Its connection to a transverse
photon branch, its softening with $f_1/S$, its positive flippability
response, and the recovery of energy through wavefront plaquettes
motivate a Feynman-type, roton-like interpretation. Independently, the
same spectra quantify previously anticipated photon interactions in a
regime where they are nonperturbative: the polarization splitting
becomes order one and $13$--$38\%$ of neutron weight leaves the photon
branches. The principal experimental message is therefore not that
the emergent photon disappears, but that finite-momentum neutron
spectroscopy of a compact spin-one-half gauge field need not resemble a
single renormalized photon pole.

